1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,000
The Japanese Fishermen
Who Fed Prewar Singapore

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,054
In the 1930s, Japanese
fishermen supplied 40 to 50 per

3
00:00:08,134 --> 00:00:12,724
cent of the fresh fish sold at
Singapore's municipal markets,

4
00:00:12,804 --> 00:00:15,863
even though there were only about a third

5
00:00:15,943 --> 00:00:20,000
as many of them as local
Chinese and Malay fishermen.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,702
Within a few years, licence
limits, a boycott and

7
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:27,870
growing suspicion had cut
the fleet down to a fraction

8
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:31,500
of its size, and by
December 1941 it was gone.

9
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,266
Japanese had been trying to
fish from Singapore since the

10
00:00:35,346 --> 00:00:40,124
late nineteenth century, but according
to the historian Hiroshi Shimizu,

11
00:00:40,204 --> 00:00:42,700
none of the early attempts succeeded.

12
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:46,520
That changed after 1913,
when Tokyo's Ministry

13
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:50,758
of Agriculture and Commerce
sent a fishery expert,

14
00:00:50,838 --> 00:00:53,975
Takayama Itarō, to survey the waters.

15
00:00:53,975 --> 00:00:58,255
His report pointed to the lack
of severe fishing restrictions,

16
00:00:58,335 --> 00:01:00,435
the large demand for fresh fish

17
00:01:00,515 --> 00:01:03,750
and the existence of
established fish markets.

18
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:08,716
Japanese fishing firms
followed in the early 1920s,

19
00:01:08,796 --> 00:01:11,090
among them Ishizu Gyogyō

20
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:15,325
Kōshi in 1921 and Taichong Kongsi in 1922.

21
00:01:15,325 --> 00:01:19,732
The men who crewed them came
mostly from Okinawa, and many from

22
00:01:19,812 --> 00:01:24,300
a single town, Itoman, on the
southern part of the main island.

23
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:29,118
Shimizu, citing an earlier Japanese
study, puts the share at about

24
00:01:29,198 --> 00:01:33,725
80 per cent of the fishermen
employed by Taichong and Ishizu.

25
00:01:33,725 --> 00:01:37,411
Itoman had almost no
farmland, and its fishermen

26
00:01:37,491 --> 00:01:40,550
had developed a method called muro-ami.

27
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:45,371
By 1920 the fishing grounds around
Okinawa were depleted and the

28
00:01:45,451 --> 00:01:50,272
island's interwar economy was
weak, while Southeast Asia offered

29
00:01:50,352 --> 00:01:55,100
warm coral waters, open sea and
a large market for fresh fish.

30
00:01:55,100 --> 00:02:00,432
The number of Okinawan men in Singapore

31
00:02:00,512 --> 00:02:05,925
rose from 292 in 1926 to 1,035 in 1937.

32
00:02:05,925 --> 00:02:08,950
Muro-ami was a team effort.

33
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:12,913
A crew of 40 to 50 fishermen
worked from two carrier

34
00:02:12,993 --> 00:02:16,725
boats and four small
wooden boats called sabani.

35
00:02:16,725 --> 00:02:21,297
Divers located a shoal of reef
fish, mainly of the genus Caesio,

36
00:02:21,377 --> 00:02:23,768
and drove it into a large bag net,

37
00:02:23,848 --> 00:02:27,700
which the men on the boats
then closed and hauled in.

38
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,425
The divers were often boys.

39
00:02:30,425 --> 00:02:34,578
Shimizu, drawing on Japanese
sources, describes how Itoman

40
00:02:34,658 --> 00:02:38,957
fishermen took boys from poor
families on Okinawa's outlying

41
00:02:39,038 --> 00:02:43,191
islands in return for an advance
payment to their parents,

42
00:02:43,271 --> 00:02:47,650
and kept them as indentured
apprentices for about ten years.

43
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:51,396
In 1929, muro-ami accounted for 600 of the

44
00:02:51,476 --> 00:02:55,403
801 Japanese fishermen
counted in Singapore,

45
00:02:55,483 --> 00:02:59,400
and for roughly 90 per
cent of their catch.

46
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:02,175
The firms financed the move.

47
00:03:02,175 --> 00:03:06,765
They recruited in Okinawa, advanced
money for travel and supplied

48
00:03:06,845 --> 00:03:11,875
boats and nets, so a skilled fisherman
with no capital could still go.

49
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:17,371
A Japanese-style fishing boat
cost about $700 in Singapore,

50
00:03:17,451 --> 00:03:20,475
and a carrier boat about $2,000.

51
00:03:20,475 --> 00:03:24,922
At Taichong Kongsi, the profit
after expenses was split 40

52
00:03:25,002 --> 00:03:29,450
per cent to the company and
60 per cent to the fishermen.

53
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:32,734
The local fishermen,
mainly Chinese and Malay,

54
00:03:32,814 --> 00:03:36,325
worked closer to shore
in small unpowered boats.

55
00:03:36,325 --> 00:03:39,926
They used stationary
stake nets called kelongs,

56
00:03:40,006 --> 00:03:41,650
drift nets and lines.

57
00:03:41,650 --> 00:03:46,370
The Japanese used both powered and
unpowered boats with modern gear,

58
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:50,050
and packed their catches
in ice to keep them fresh.

59
00:03:50,050 --> 00:03:55,686
A 1933 report by the colonial
Director of Fisheries, W. Birtwistle,

60
00:03:55,766 --> 00:04:00,293
gave the average catch of a
Chinese or Malay drift-net

61
00:04:00,373 --> 00:04:06,175
fisherman as 15 katties of fish a
day, against 150 for the Japanese.

62
00:04:06,175 --> 00:04:09,900
A katti is about 680 grams.

63
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:14,677
The colonial Blue Book for
1936 and the Malayan Year Book

64
00:04:14,757 --> 00:04:19,193
for 1939 both put the Japanese
share of the fish sold

65
00:04:19,273 --> 00:04:23,875
at Singapore's municipal
markets at 40 to 50 per cent.

66
00:04:23,875 --> 00:04:26,850
The Depression hit the fleet hard.

67
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:31,542
In the early 1930s, fish
prices fell to about a quarter

68
00:04:31,622 --> 00:04:36,747
of their earlier level, partly
because of a Chinese boycott,

69
00:04:36,827 --> 00:04:41,432
and the average monthly
income of a muro-ami fisherman

70
00:04:41,512 --> 00:04:45,850
dropped from $70 in the
late 1920s to $25 in 1932.

71
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:49,836
Firms shifted toward drift-net
fishing for Spanish mackerel,

72
00:04:49,916 --> 00:04:53,575
whose price fell less steeply,
and several went under.

73
00:04:53,575 --> 00:04:58,375
Ishizu Gyogyō Kōshi went bankrupt in 1936.

74
00:04:58,375 --> 00:05:02,596
Taichong Kongsi took
over boats, gear and men

75
00:05:02,676 --> 00:05:07,087
from the failing firms,
and by 1936 it employed

76
00:05:07,167 --> 00:05:11,850
671 of the 1,038 Japanese
fishermen in Singapore.

77
00:05:11,850 --> 00:05:17,300
It built an ice factory and
cold-storage plant in 1935.

78
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:21,817
In 1937 its owner, Eifuku Tora,
reorganised the business as

79
00:05:21,897 --> 00:05:25,401
a holding company that
also covered ironworks,

80
00:05:25,481 --> 00:05:31,325
shipbuilding and a farm, and he built
a pier with oil tanks at Tanjong Rhu.

81
00:05:31,325 --> 00:05:36,561
The Japanese community in Singapore
had begun with a very different group,

82
00:05:36,641 --> 00:05:40,441
the karayuki-san women of
the late nineteenth century,

83
00:05:40,521 --> 00:05:44,975
whose story is told in our post
on the Japanese Cemetery Park.

84
00:05:44,975 --> 00:05:48,816
As war in Asia approached,
the colonial authorities

85
00:05:48,896 --> 00:05:53,125
grew more concerned about
Japanese nationals in Malaya.

86
00:05:53,125 --> 00:05:56,970
British officials had
suspected since the 1920s that

87
00:05:57,050 --> 00:06:00,743
Japanese fishing boats
might be gathering military

88
00:06:00,823 --> 00:06:05,050
information around Singapore's
naval base and airfields.

89
00:06:05,050 --> 00:06:09,453
Japanese sources cited by
Shimizu record 40 cases

90
00:06:09,533 --> 00:06:13,753
between 1935 and 1940 in
which Japanese fishing

91
00:06:13,833 --> 00:06:18,225
vessels strayed into
British territorial waters.

92
00:06:18,225 --> 00:06:22,075
The evidence for spying
is harder to pin down.

93
00:06:22,075 --> 00:06:26,121
Japanese intelligence activity
in prewar Malaya is documented,

94
00:06:26,201 --> 00:06:29,649
but the historian Max
Everest-Phillips found that the

95
00:06:29,729 --> 00:06:32,976
scale of the threat was
never clearly established,

96
00:06:33,056 --> 00:06:37,302
and the National Library Board's
account of the period notes that

97
00:06:37,382 --> 00:06:41,575
accusations against the fishermen
were rarely, if ever, proven.

98
00:06:41,575 --> 00:06:45,250
The suspicion nevertheless shaped policy.

99
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:48,025
The restrictions came in steps.

100
00:06:48,025 --> 00:06:51,193
From February 1937, no new licences

101
00:06:51,273 --> 00:06:54,800
were issued to Japanese fishing boats.

102
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:59,279
In 1938, the licences of 30
of about 150 Japanese-owned

103
00:06:59,359 --> 00:07:03,838
boats were not renewed, and
Japanese owners were barred

104
00:07:03,918 --> 00:07:08,725
from registering boats under
the names of local residents.

105
00:07:08,725 --> 00:07:12,488
In February 1939, the
licences of 20 power boats

106
00:07:12,568 --> 00:07:16,092
and three sailing boats
belonging to Eifuku's

107
00:07:16,172 --> 00:07:19,775
group and seven other
firms were not renewed.

108
00:07:19,775 --> 00:07:23,767
The authorities then
gave notice that licences

109
00:07:23,847 --> 00:07:28,450
expiring from July 1939
would not be renewed either.

110
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:30,819
Thirty Japanese fishing

111
00:07:30,899 --> 00:07:34,200
companies had operated in 1938.

112
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:36,671
In 1940 only three remained:

113
00:07:36,751 --> 00:07:41,125
Taichong Kongsi, Ōshiro
Gumi and Kaneshiro Gumi.

114
00:07:41,125 --> 00:07:45,250
A trade dispute ran
alongside the licensing.

115
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:48,623
After the Sino-Japanese War began in 1937,

116
00:07:48,703 --> 00:07:52,488
Singapore's fishmongers
began openly boycotting

117
00:07:52,568 --> 00:07:56,350
Japanese-caught fish in
December of that year.

118
00:07:56,350 --> 00:08:00,689
On the 30th of January 1938,
The Sunday Times reported that

119
00:08:00,769 --> 00:08:05,182
Japanese boats were taking their
catches to Batavia instead,

120
00:08:05,262 --> 00:08:10,200
and that fish prices in Singapore
had risen by a few cents a catty,

121
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:14,025
but that the feared shortage
had not materialised.

122
00:08:14,025 --> 00:08:17,600
Fishermen and firms
felt the effect quickly.

123
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:22,253
According to a petition reported
in The Sunday Times on the 2nd

124
00:08:22,333 --> 00:08:26,762
of April 1939, the Japanese had
supplied half of Singapore's

125
00:08:26,842 --> 00:08:31,575
fish before the boycott and still
supplied more than a quarter.

126
00:08:31,575 --> 00:08:35,218
The number of Japanese
fishermen had fallen

127
00:08:35,298 --> 00:08:39,028
from about 1,400 to
about 900 within a year,

128
00:08:39,108 --> 00:08:43,350
and their power boats from
about 100 to about 50.

129
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:46,608
Some of the boats had
gone to Japanese waters

130
00:08:46,688 --> 00:08:50,025
and some of the men to
work in Malayan mines.

131
00:08:50,025 --> 00:08:54,550
The petitioners asked the government
to lift the licence restriction

132
00:08:54,630 --> 00:08:58,625
and said it favoured domiciled
Chinese and Malay fishermen.

133
00:08:58,625 --> 00:09:03,418
The government's own line, in the
Director of Fisheries' annual report,

134
00:09:03,498 --> 00:09:05,270
was that licences for local

135
00:09:05,350 --> 00:09:09,125
fishing methods were to be
confined to local fishermen.

136
00:09:09,125 --> 00:09:12,600
Some of the displaced
men found other work.

137
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:16,367
By late 1938, more than
500 Japanese fishermen

138
00:09:16,447 --> 00:09:20,715
were employed at the Bukit
Besi iron mine at Dungun,

139
00:09:20,795 --> 00:09:24,725
on the Malayan east coast,
run by Nippon Kōgyō.

140
00:09:24,725 --> 00:09:30,851
Some 2,300 Chinese miners had quit it
in protest at the Sino-Japanese War,

141
00:09:30,931 --> 00:09:33,950
and the company needed replacements.

142
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:38,709
Others left Singapore for other
ports, and by 1940 the fleet that

143
00:09:38,789 --> 00:09:43,925
had supplied close to half the city's
fresh fish was a small remnant.

144
00:09:43,925 --> 00:09:47,438
When war reached Malaya in December 1941,

145
00:09:47,518 --> 00:09:51,550
Japanese civilians in
Singapore were interned.

146
00:09:51,550 --> 00:09:56,715
According to the historians
Christine de Matos and Rowena Ward,

147
00:09:56,795 --> 00:10:01,211
nearly 2,700 Japanese civilians
and colonial subjects,

148
00:10:01,291 --> 00:10:05,041
mainly from Singapore
and Malaya, were held in

149
00:10:05,121 --> 00:10:09,950
camps in British India between
December 1941 and May 1946.

150
00:10:09,950 --> 00:10:14,075
One boat from the fleet
had a remarkable afterlife.

151
00:10:14,075 --> 00:10:18,188
Kofuku Maru, a Japanese
fishing vessel built

152
00:10:18,268 --> 00:10:21,143
in 1934 and based in Singapore,

153
00:10:21,223 --> 00:10:26,750
was captured by the American
destroyer USS Edsall in 1941.

154
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:30,979
In Allied hands, she carried
more than 1,100 people from

155
00:10:31,059 --> 00:10:34,903
sunken ships off Sumatra
to safety, made her way to

156
00:10:34,983 --> 00:10:39,600
Australia by way of Ceylon and
India, and was renamed Krait.

157
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:43,962
In September 1943 she carried
commandos of the Z Special

158
00:10:44,042 --> 00:10:47,849
Unit to the waters near
Singapore, disguised as a

159
00:10:47,929 --> 00:10:52,450
Japanese fishing boat, in the
operation known as Jaywick.

160
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:54,769
Their canoeists raided the harbour,

161
00:10:54,849 --> 00:10:58,275
and the raid is credited
with sinking seven ships.

162
00:10:58,275 --> 00:11:01,410
A Japanese fishing boat
had been an ordinary

163
00:11:01,490 --> 00:11:04,925
sight in those waters
only a few years earlier.

164
00:11:04,925 --> 00:11:08,518
Why it matters today: The
Japanese fishing fleet rarely

165
00:11:08,598 --> 00:11:12,191
appears in the story Singapore
tells about its own food

166
00:11:12,271 --> 00:11:15,864
supply and its prewar economy,
although for a few years

167
00:11:15,944 --> 00:11:19,750
it supplied close to half the
fish at the city's markets.

168
00:11:19,750 --> 00:11:25,175
It also shows how the approach of
war reshaped an ordinary trade.

169
00:11:25,175 --> 00:11:28,040
Within about three years, licence limits,

170
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:32,207
a boycott and suspicion had
cut roughly 30 firms to three,

171
00:11:32,287 --> 00:11:36,525
and a boat from the same fleet
ended up on the Allied side.
